Three bytes at position 0 turned every field extractor off, and the hook that reads them fails open. A UTF-8 BOM sits at byte 0, INSIDE line 1, so a `^`-anchored `**Key**:` grep stops matching: key on line 1 behind a BOM scored 0, the same key on line 2 scored 1, no BOM on line 1 scored 1. The server strips a BOM before HASHING and the hooks did not before GREPPING, so the same file was two different files depending on which subsystem was looking — and "no **Gate** found" is pre-commit-test.sh's warn-and-allow arm, a silently ungated commit rather than a parse error. Tenth fail-open path, and routine rather than exotic: PowerShell 5.1's `>` and `Out-File` write UTF-8 WITH a BOM, so one redirect gives a Windows consumer's config file a permanent invisible one; a consumer's PROJECT_CONTEXT.md was found carrying one, harmless only because their keys sit on lines 14 and 29. Fixed as a CLASS, because this class has been patched one site at a time three times here: one constant, GC_KEY_PRE in hooks/lib/git-cmd.sh, carries the BOM tolerance next to the existing list-marker tolerance with the explanation above it, and all five extractors go through it (**Gate** in run-gate.sh and gate-before-merge.sh, **Test** and **Gate** in pre-commit-test.sh, **Protected branches** in git-cmd.sh); the paired sed needed no change since its leading `.*` eats the BOM with the marker. run-gate.sh stays standalone — it must run with no JSON parser on PATH, which sourcing git-cmd.sh forbids — so it repeats the literal, and verify-template-consistency.sh now asserts both that the two copies agree and that EVERY `grep -E` over PROJECT_CONTEXT.md in hooks/ goes through GC_KEY_PRE; that census is the part that stops the next instance. The fixtures are a pair by POSITION (line 1 and line 2) crossed with a pair by POLARITY, and both pairs are load-bearing: line 2 is the shape every consumer file actually has and passed before the fix, so the regression guarded is "someone moved the key up", and a line-1-only arm would pass a partial fix that only strips a BOM immediately followed by the key; while the failing arms cannot discriminate alone, because a partial fix leaving the BOM glued to the value runs `bash -c '<BOM>false'`, command-not-found, also a block — the passing arms carry it, asserted on the ABSENCE of the no-Test/Gate WARN via the new check_nomsg helper. Removing a BOM is hash-NEUTRAL (the server strips it before hashing: 187d06eb… with and without, versus a390c69d… for a naive raw sha256), so a consumer can delete three bytes with zero manifest churn; `head -c 3 PROJECT_CONTEXT.md | od -An -tx1` says whether they have one. sync-template loses the mandatory client-side re-hash from step 7: it is not implementable as written and reddens PROJECT_CONTEXT.md first, the one file the skill's own conflict guidance says every consumer must splice, leaving only two bad answers — finalize anyway and learn to ignore the guard, or stop a clean sync. localHash is sha256 over Python universal-newline text with a leading BOM stripped and stripped again after re-encoding; a consumer ruled out raw sha256, newline normalisation, whitespace stripping and placeholder reversal in all six permutations before the BOM was identified. The hand-typed-hash incident stays covered server-side (template_finalize_sync rejects malformed hashes) and by the build-programmatically rule; the post-finalize template_compute_status check stays, and the definition survives only as an optional diagnostic. Step 2b's `\r` hazard is one layer earlier than v2.2.3 documented — it is a hazard on any path list that passes through Python text mode, and Method 2, presented as the works-everywhere alternative, is the MORE exposed one because its path source is left to the implementer; Method 1 escapes only by building bytes inside Python. Step 7 also now acts on the predates-part-hash hint: those files join the sync's source="skip" set, re-registered last, which reclassified a consumer's CLAUDE.md to